WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.729
Hi, James Murphy here.

00:00:03.729 --> 00:00:07.517
Back with another new feature in Python 3.9

00:00:07.517 --> 00:00:11.139
Today, we're going to be talking about type hinting.

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:14.095
So, let's just get to it.

00:00:14.095 --> 00:00:18.881
In previous versions of Python, if you wanted to type hint, say, a list,

00:00:18.881 --> 00:00:22.514
 well, you could do it like this.

00:00:22.514 --> 00:00:27.431
You could say x is a capital list of, let's just say, integers.

00:00:27.431 --> 00:00:33.500
Now, capital list we're going to have to import from the typing module.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:41.714
And that's because, the built-in list didn't previously allow parameterization like this.

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:45.000
I couldn't say a lowercase list of int.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.059
That would just be a syntax error.

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:50.997
But in Python 3.9, that's exactly what they've introduced.

00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:54.620
So, this syntax here is now deprecated.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:00.834
And instead, you should be using just the regular old list.

00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:03.808
And it looks like that.

00:01:03.808 --> 00:01:10.395
You can also actually use the list on the other side and it's the same thing.

00:01:10.395 --> 00:01:18.473
So, I could say x is list int and then instantiate it that way.

00:01:18.473 --> 00:01:26.831
Note however, that list int is actually what's called a generic alias.

00:01:26.831 --> 00:01:35.096
So, the type of x, if I run this program here. Well, let's actually print it.

00:01:41.178 --> 00:01:47.638
Even if I instantiated it this way, the type of x is still just list.

00:01:47.638 --> 00:01:53.240
However, list of int is not just list.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:02:01.747
So, if I actually look at the type list of int and compare that to list,

00:02:01.747 --> 00:02:05.378
this is actually going to be false.

00:02:05.378 --> 00:02:07.502
Let's just run that.

00:02:07.502 --> 00:02:11.294
You can see that those two are different types.

00:02:11.294 --> 00:02:14.342
Where, of course, if I compare it to itself,

00:02:14.342 --> 00:02:17.497
then it is true. It's not just that it's not comparable.

00:02:20.208 --> 00:02:24.739
So, let's just put that back. So, it's false again.

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:31.331
And we can actually get that int parameter at runtime if we want it.

00:02:31.331 --> 00:02:33.833
So, you can actually see it.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:41.006
Because if I just print out the type itself, not the variable x, but this generic alias type,

00:02:41.006 --> 00:02:44.430
you can see that it does show up as list of int.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.408
So, that means that that information is there stored somewhere at runtime.

00:02:48.408 --> 00:02:51.730
And it's actually stored in this origin parameter.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:57.563
So, if I have my type t to be list of int,

00:02:57.563 --> 00:03:02.640
then the double under origin variable

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.900
which is highlighted because my text editor doesn't know about it yet.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.402
Because Python 3.9 is still new.

00:03:11.402 --> 00:03:16.898
That variable will point back to the base class, kind of.

00:03:16.898 --> 00:03:20.892
It's not really the base class. List of int doesn't inherit from list.

00:03:20.892 --> 00:03:23.807
But it points to just the list part of it

00:03:23.807 --> 00:03:26.381
and...

00:03:26.381 --> 00:03:31.648
the args parameter.

00:03:31.648 --> 00:03:32.844
Let's just put that up here.

00:03:32.844 --> 00:03:42.407
The args parameter will give you a list of things back that you put into here.

00:03:42.407 --> 00:03:46.068
Now with list, there's just one thing.

00:03:46.068 --> 00:03:46.962
So, it's a little bit weird.

00:03:46.962 --> 00:03:51.308
This notation here actually means this is a tuple with one thing in it

00:03:51.308 --> 00:03:56.393
which is the type int. Which is exactly what we put in here.

00:03:56.393 --> 00:04:02.504
But if you have something like a dictionary

00:04:02.504 --> 00:04:04.668
and I type hinted it with

00:04:04.668 --> 00:04:11.831
Now, I can use lowercase dict of str and int, let's say.

00:04:11.831 --> 00:04:19.010
Well, this type dict of str and int

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:26.416
I can now get back that str and int as a tuple using the args method.

00:04:26.416 --> 00:04:30.100
The args attribute, excuse me.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.936
So, we can see that when we run it.

00:04:31.936 --> 00:04:39.863
Now, the last line there shows the str and int classes.

00:04:39.863 --> 00:04:42.427
This also works if you have parameterized types.

00:04:42.427 --> 00:04:49.392
So, if I have a type variable T,

00:04:49.392 --> 00:04:54.027
which I will have to import from typing.

00:04:54.027 --> 00:04:55.905
And just so you know how it prints.

00:04:55.905 --> 00:04:59.697
You can see that it comes up with this tilde T.

00:04:59.697 --> 00:05:08.040
So, that's a type that I have now created as a generic placeholder for a type.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:17.973
If I make my little type t be dict of str to T

00:05:17.973 --> 00:05:22.871
and then print t out,

00:05:22.871 --> 00:05:26.427
you'll see that that type variable T is in there.

00:05:26.427 --> 00:05:34.110
And I can access it at runtime using the dunder parameters attribute.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.504
So, we can see that as follows.

00:05:44.751 --> 00:05:48.573
And that's going to give me back just a list of the type variables.

00:05:48.573 --> 00:05:51.275
So, notice str is not in there.

00:05:54.551 --> 00:06:04.564
Finally, let's look at some things that we can or can't do with this new generic alias.

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:12.255
So, one of the things you might want to do is a runtime check of the type.

00:06:12.255 --> 00:06:20.901
So, you might think: Is it possible to check isinstance?

00:06:20.901 --> 00:06:28.467
So, is some list an instance of list event?

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.000
And you can see that there are red squiggles here.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:46.733
So, my editor is correct that there's an error but it's not correct about what the error is.

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:57.047
The actual error here is that you can't use in isinstance with a parameterized generic type.

00:06:57.047 --> 00:07:04.347
So, if you want to check to see if something is an instance of list of int,

00:07:04.347 --> 00:07:07.800
meaning, check to see if all of its elements are integers,

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:13.218
then you just have to check all of its elements individually.

00:07:13.218 --> 00:07:19.490
So, this mechanism is actually a thin proxy layer on top of the existing list type.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:28.742
All it does is redirect all of the calls and method accesses and everything to the underlying list type.

00:07:28.742 --> 00:07:39.830
And that's why, except for origin args, parameters and of course, the square brackets getitem.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.840
But that's all. Everything else is forward directly to list.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:50.600
And if you scroll back up with me to this line 8, that's actually why this line works,

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.263
and why the type of x is just list.

00:07:52.263 --> 00:08:01.098
Because it's just proxying that method call, the constructor, to the underlying list type.

00:08:04.487 --> 00:08:10.000
So, let's go ahead and comment out this line because we now know that that's not allowed.

00:08:12.606 --> 00:08:16.721
You also can't check to see if something is a subclass this way.

00:08:16.721 --> 00:08:21.363
So, if I wanted to check,

00:08:21.363 --> 00:08:26.286
if...

00:08:26.286 --> 00:08:32.622
the class list, say, is a subclass of list of int,

00:08:32.622 --> 00:08:36.679
you might think that that would be true. But in fact, it's not

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.667
for the same reasons as the previous example.

00:08:39.667 --> 00:08:44.159
So, if we print this, this is again a type error

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:51.196
and you see that they say that the second argument can't be a parameterized generic.

00:08:51.196 --> 00:09:04.237
So basically, you're not allowed to check if something is a subclass of or an instance of one of these generic aliases.

00:09:04.237 --> 00:09:10.449
But one thing you can do is, you can check to see if a type is a generic alias

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:15.212
just by using is instance with generic alias.

00:09:15.212 --> 00:09:25.978
So, we can check isinstance.

00:09:25.978 --> 00:09:31.852
We can check to see if list of int is a generic alias.

00:09:31.852 --> 00:09:36.620
And we're going to have to import this from types.

00:09:38.861 --> 00:09:42.333
You can see with this that indeed that's true.

00:09:45.139 --> 00:09:48.758
So, that's all that I really wanted to show

00:09:48.758 --> 00:09:56.894
about the new generic aliases which allow type hinting of generics and standard collections.

